!deck: Math::Algebra
!tags: Math Algebra Inequality
!type: Basic Math

!id: mkbLig7hww
!front: Explain why if \( |x| \le c \), \( c \ge 0 \) is true then \( x \le c \) is true as well.
!back:
If \( x \) is non-negative, then \( |x| = x \), so \( x \le c \).

If \( x \) is negative, then \( |x| = -x \), so \( -x \le c \) which implies \( x \ge -c \). Since \( c \ge 0 \), we have \( -c \le c \), so \( x \le c \).
~~~

!id: OFMTtrXppe
!front: Explain why if \( x \le c \), \( c \ge 0 \) is true then \( |x| \le c \) is not necessarily true as well.
!back: Counterexample: Let \( x = -2 \), \( |x| = 2 \) and \( c = 1 \). Then \( -2 \le 1 \) is true, but \( 2 \not \le 1 \).
~~~

!id: nQgWncXCnf
!front: Explain why \( -|x| \le x \le |x| \).
!back:
If \( x \) is non-negative, then \( |x| = x \), so \( -|x| = -x \le x \le x = |x| \).

If \( x \) is negative, then \( |x| = -x \), so \( -|x| = x \le x \le -x = |x| \).
~~~

!id: kffGeUSWDK
!front: Solve \( |x| \le c \), \( c \ge 0 \).
!back:
\[ -c \le x \le c \]
\[ x \in [-c, c] \]
~~~

!id: um6DlBSRjb
!front: Solve \( |x| \ge c \), \( c \ge 0 \).
!back:
\[ x \le -c \text{ or } x \ge c \]
\[ x \in \left( -\infty, -c \right] \cup \left[ c, \infty \right) \]
~~~

!id: B8fZ2E9XKm
!front: Solve \( |x - a| \le c \), \( c \ge 0 \).
!back:
\[ -c \le x - a \le c \]
\[ a - c \le x \le a + c \]
\[ x \in [a - c, a + c] \]
~~~

!id: H7Z0dQ5N2m
!front: Solve \( |x - a| \ge c \), \( c \ge 0 \).
!back:
\[ x - a \le -c \quad \text{or} \quad x - a \ge c \]
\[ x \le a - c \quad \text{or} \quad x \ge a + c \]
\[ x \in (-\infty, a-c] \cup [a+c, \infty) \]
~~~

!id: HyckbDIJks
!front: Solve \( x^2 \le c \), \( c \ge 0 \).
!back:
\[
\begin{align*}
    x^2 & \le c \\
    \sqrt{x^2} & \le \sqrt{c} \\
    |x| & \le \sqrt{c}
\end{align*}
\]

The solution is

\[ -\sqrt{c} \le x \le \sqrt{c} \]
\[ x \in \left[ -\sqrt{c}, \sqrt{c} \right] \]
~~~

!id: u2C6X3GATT
!front: Solve \( x^2 \ge c \), \( c \ge 0 \).
!back:
\[
\begin{align*}
    x^2 & \ge c \\
    \sqrt{x^2} & \ge \sqrt{c} \\
    |x| & \ge \sqrt{c}
\end{align*}
\]

The solution is

\[ x \le -\sqrt{c} \text{ or } x \ge \sqrt{c} \]
\[ x \in \left( -\infty, -\sqrt{c} \right] \cup \left[ \sqrt{c}, \infty \right) \]
~~~

!id: eqpPfVf5Hl
!front: True or false: From \( (a + b)^2 \ge 0 \) can we conclude that \( a + b \ge 0 \).
!back: False. The correct conclusion is \( |x + y| \ge 0 \).
~~~

!id: WiB0PQAE77
!front: True or false: If \( a \le b \), then \( a^2 \le b^2 \)
!back:
False in general.

Counterexample: Let \( a = -3 \), \( b = 2 \), then \( -3 \le 2 \) but \( 9 \not \le 4 \).
~~~

!id: T8cM3K6n5A
!front: True or false: If \( |a| \le |b| \) then \( a^2 \le b^2 \).
!back:
True, because

\[ |a| \le |b| \Rightarrow |a|^2 \le |b|^2 \Rightarrow a^2 \le b^2 \]
~~~

!id: kxxCWMfyuv
!front: If \( a^2 \le b^2 \), what inequality does this imply about \( |a| \) and \( |b| \)?
!back: \[ |a| \le |b| \]
~~~

!id: r6KWkBNdxO
!front: When solving an inequality \( a \le b \), what happens with the inequality direction if we add a constant \( c \) to both sides?
!back: Inequality direction stays the same.
~~~

!id: 1X6H3r9RBy
!front: When solving an inequality \( a \le b \), what happens with the inequality direction if we multiply both sides by a positive constant \( c > 0 \)?
!back: Inequality direction stays the same.
~~~

!id: sYtII2Ud8s
!front: When solving an inequality \( a \le b \), what happens with the inequality direction if we multiply both sides by a negative constant \( c < 0 \)?
!back: Inequality direction flips.
~~~

!id: 4y0AWLBZXN
!front: If \( a \le b \) and \( f \) is an increasing function, what happens to the inequality after applying \( f \)?
!back:
It stays the same:

\[ f(a) \le f(b) \]

![Increasing vs. decreasing function: inequality direction](img/monotonic-direction.svg)
~~~

!id: 38YVrjoIzc
!front: If \( a \le b \) and \( f \) is a decreasing function, what happens to the inequality after applying \( f \)?
!back:
The direction flips:

\[ f(a) \ge f(b) \]

![Increasing vs. decreasing function: inequality direction](img/monotonic-direction.svg)
~~~

!id: HDnjnyqxHz
!front: If \( 0 < a \le b \), what inequality holds between \( 1/a \) and \( 1/b \)?
!back: \( 1/a \ge 1/b \) (reciprocal is decreasing for positive numbers).
~~~

!id: PRAqJhDlb8
!front: Is it true that if \( a > b \) then \( \ln(a) > \ln(b) \)? Why?
!back:
Yes, if \( a > 0 \) and \( b > 0 \) then

\[ a > b \implies \ln(a) > \ln(b) \]

because \( \ln(x) \) is monotonic and strictly increasing.
~~~

!id: MTp5E7svOr
!front: Is it true that if \( a > b \) then \( e^a > e^b \)? Why?
!back:
Yes, for any \( a \) and \( b \)

\[ a > b \implies e^a > e^b \]

because \( e^x \) is monotonic and strictly increasing.
~~~

!id: Z7P2A8W9YJ
!front: True or false: If \( a \le b \) then \( a^3 \le b^3 \).
!back: True, because \( f(x) = x^3 \) is strictly increasing on \( \mathbb{R} \).
~~~

!id: Kt1X3Lw8aQ
!front: Prove that \( |x| \ge 0 \) for all real \( x \).
!back:
By definition,

\[
|x| =
\begin{cases}
    x, & x \ge 0 \\
    -x, & x < 0
\end{cases}
\]

In both cases the value is non-negative, hence

\[ |x| \ge 0 \]
~~~

!id: rPac9OHGod
!front: Prove the inequality \( ab \le |a| |b| \).
!back:
- Case 1: \( a,b \ge 0 \). Then \( |a|=a \), \( |b|=b \), so \( ab=|a||b| \).
- Case 2: \( a,b \le 0 \). Then \( |a|=-a \), \( |b|=-b \), so \( ab=(-a)(-b)=|a||b| \).
- Case 3: \( a \) and \( b \) have opposite signs. Say \( a \ge 0 \), \( b \le 0 \). Then \( ab \le 0 \), but \( |a||b| \ge 0 \). So automatically \( ab \le |a||b| \). Same if \( a \le 0 \), \( b \ge 0 \).
~~~

!id: QsX3w9Q5h0
!front: What is the triangle inequality?
!back:
The triangle inequality states that for any real numbers \( a \) and \( b \):

\[ |a + b| \leq |a| + |b| \]
~~~

!id: ASfKxxbdiF
!front: Give a geometric interpretation of the triangle inequality for vectors \( \mathbf{a},\mathbf{b}\in\mathbb{R}^2 \).
!back:
Place \( \mathbf{a} \) and \( \mathbf{b} \) head to tail. Their sum is the direct displacement from the starting point to the endpoint. The direct segment cannot be longer than the two-segment path, so
\[ \|\mathbf{a}+\mathbf{b}\| \le \|\mathbf{a}\|+\|\mathbf{b}\|. \]

![Vectors a and b placed head to tail, with the direct segment representing their sum and no longer than the two-segment path](img/triangle-inequality.svg)
~~~

!id: S9D2P7B8QX
!front: What is the reverse triangle inequality?
!back:
The reverse triangle inequality states that for any real numbers \( a \) and \( b \):

\[ ||a| - |b|| \le |a - b| \]
~~~

!id: F7Z3B5R2XQ
!front: What is the AM–GM inequality?
!back:
The AM–GM inequality states that for for any real number \( a,b \ge 0 \):

\[ \frac{a+b}{2} \ge \sqrt{ab} \]

Equality holds iff \( a=b \).
~~~

!id: eZ7UTdA98y
!front: Give a geometric interpretation of the AM–GM inequality for \( a,b \ge 0 \) using a semicircle.
!back:
Split a semicircle's diameter into lengths \( a \) and \( b \). The perpendicular from the split point to the semicircle has length \( \sqrt{ab} \), while the radius is \( (a+b)/2 \). That perpendicular is no longer than the radius, giving \( \sqrt{ab} \le (a+b)/2 \), with equality when \( a=b \).

![A semicircle with diameter split into lengths a and b, showing the geometric mean as a perpendicular height and the arithmetic mean as the radius](img/am-gm-semicircle.svg)
~~~

!id: HoDhG80bGb
!front: Prove the AM-GM inequality \( \frac{a+b}{2} \ge \sqrt{ab} \) for \( a, b \ge 0 \).
!back:
\[
\begin{align*}
    (a-b)^2 & \ge 0 \\
    a^2 - 2ab + b^2 & \ge 0 \\
    a^2 - 2ab + 4ab + b^2 & \ge 4ab \\
    a^2 + 2ab + b^2 & \ge 4ab \\
    (a+b)^2 & \ge 4ab \\
    \frac{a+b}{2} & \ge \sqrt{ab}
\end{align*}
\]

Equality holds iff \( a=b \).
~~~

!id: N9B8F6D2XW
!front: If \( 0 \le a \le b \), what can we say about \( a^n \) and \( b^n \) for integer \( n \ge 1 \)?
!back:
\[ 0 \le a^n \le b^n \]

Powers preserve order for non-negative numbers.
~~~

!id: KZP4F2M9A8
!front: Prove that \( x^2 + 1 \ge 2x \) for all real \( x \).
!back:
Rearrange:

\[ x^2 - 2x + 1 \ge 0 \]

\[ (x-1)^2 \ge 0 \]

which is always true.
~~~

!id: H8M4Q9A5RZ
!front: Prove that \( a^2 + b^2 \ge 2ab \).
!back: \[ a^2 + b^2 - 2ab = (a-b)^2 \ge 0 \]
~~~

!id: E6RZ0M2P5A
!front: Prove that \( (a-b)^2 \ge 0 \) implies \( a^2 + b^2 \ge 2ab \).
!back:
Expand:

\[ (a-b)^2 = a^2 - 2ab + b^2 \ge 0 \]

Rearrange:

\[ a^2 + b^2 \ge 2ab \]
~~~

!id: CWAazZMbtb
!front: Prove that \( |a - b| \le |a| + |b| \) for any real \( a, b \).
!back:
Apply the triangle inequality with \( b \) replaced by \( -b \):

\[
\begin{align*}
|a - b| &= |a + (-b)| \\
        &\le |a| + |-b| \\
        &= |a| + |b|
\end{align*}
\]
~~~

!id: yAqWUYuXhd
!front: Prove that \( \big| |a| - |b| \big| \le |a + b| \) for any real \( a, b \).
!back:
Apply the reverse triangle inequality with \( b \) replaced by \( -b \):

\[ \big| |a| - |-b| \big| \le |a - (-b)| \]

Since \( |-b| = |b| \) and \( a - (-b) = a + b \), this becomes

\[ \big| |a| - |b| \big| \le |a + b| \]
~~~

!id: 8dY5wY36R2
!front: Prove that \( |a| \le |a - b| + |b| \) for any real \( a, b \).
!back:
Write \( a = (a-b) + b \), then apply the triangle inequality:

\[ |a| = |(a-b)+b| \le |a-b| + |b| \]
!extra: This "add and subtract a middle term" step is the key trick behind most bounding arguments in analysis, e.g. bounding \( |f(x)| \) via \( |f(x) - L| + |L| \).
~~~

!id: GGbwzkEeEu
!front: Prove that \( |a+b+c| \le |a| + |b| + |c| \) for any real \( a, b, c \).
!back:
Apply the triangle inequality twice, first to \( (a+b) \) and \( c \), then to \( a \) and \( b \):

\[
\begin{align*}
|a+b+c| &= |(a+b)+c| \\
        &\le |a+b| + |c| \\
        &\le |a| + |b| + |c|
\end{align*}
\]
!extra: The same argument extends by induction to any finite sum: \( |a_1 + \cdots + a_n| \le |a_1| + \cdots + |a_n| \).
~~~

!id: BQzNTeo42g
!front: Prove that \( \sqrt{a+b} \le \sqrt{a} + \sqrt{b} \) for \( a, b \ge 0 \).
!back:
Both sides are non-negative, so it suffices to prove the squared inequality:

\[ (\sqrt{a+b})^2 \le (\sqrt{a}+\sqrt{b})^2 \]

\[
\begin{align*}
a+b &\le a + 2\sqrt{ab} + b \\
0 &\le 2\sqrt{ab}
\end{align*}
\]

which holds because \( \sqrt{ab} \ge 0 \) for \( a, b \ge 0 \).

![Right triangle with legs sqrt(a) and sqrt(b) meeting at a right angle, and hypotenuse sqrt(a+b) by the Pythagorean theorem, showing the claim is the ordinary triangle inequality applied to this triangle](img/sqrt-triangle-inequality.svg)
~~~

!id: kuEXqIBdRo
!front: What is Bernoulli's inequality?
!back:
Bernoulli's inequality states that for any real \( x \ge -1 \) and integer \( n \ge 1 \):

\[ (1+x)^n \ge 1 + nx \]

Equality holds iff \( x = 0 \) or \( n = 1 \).
~~~

!id: YUDOcdNKEh
!front: Give a geometric interpretation of Bernoulli's inequality for \( x \ge -1 \) and integer \( n \ge 1 \).
!back:
For \( f(x)=(1+x)^n \), the tangent at \( x=0 \) is \( y=1+nx \). When \( n \ge 2 \), the graph is convex on \( [-1,\infty) \), so it lies on or above that tangent, touching it only at \( x=0 \).

![Plot of (1+x)^n for n=3 and its tangent at x=0, with the curve above the tangent](img/bernoulli-inequality.svg)

When \( n=1 \), the graph and tangent coincide.
~~~

!id: ormoCYzPJz
!front: Prove Bernoulli's inequality \( (1+x)^n \ge 1 + nx \) for \( x \ge -1 \), integer \( n \ge 1 \).
!back:
Proof by induction on \( n \).

- Base case \( n = 1 \): \( (1+x)^1 = 1+x = 1 + 1 \cdot x \), so equality holds.
- Inductive step: assume \( (1+x)^k \ge 1+kx \) for some \( k \ge 1 \). Since \( x \ge -1 \), \( 1+x \ge 0 \), so multiplying both sides of the inductive hypothesis by \( 1+x \) preserves the inequality:

\[
\begin{align*}
(1+x)^{k+1} &= (1+x)^k(1+x) \\
            &\ge (1+kx)(1+x) \\
            &= 1 + (k+1)x + kx^2 \\
            &\ge 1 + (k+1)x
\end{align*}
\]

since \( kx^2 \ge 0 \).
~~~
